    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and all 3 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the optional, city-region-validated owner `postcode`:\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)** \u2014 added an optional `postcode` string field to `OwnerFields` with pattern `^[0-9]{4}$`. Since it's not in `required`, absent postcodes stay accepted (backward-compatible), and bean validation rejects malformed values (e.g. `\"12\"`) with 400. Because `Owner` includes `OwnerFields`, the generated `OwnerDto` returns `postcode`.\n\n**Model (`Owner.java`)** \u2014 added a `postcode` column with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it both ways by name, so it's stored on create and returned on read with no mapper changes.\n\n**Region-range rule (`util/PostcodeValidator.java`)** \u2014 a pure function mirroring `LocalityResolver`: absent postcode is valid; a present one must be 4 digits and, when the city maps to a known region (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), fall in range; a city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n\n**Controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`)** \u2014 added `rejectInvalidPostcode`, throwing the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` (\u2192 400) when a present postcode is out of range for the city's region.\n\n**Schemas** \u2014 added the `postcode` column to all four DDL files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); the test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in `schema.sql`.\n\nAll three acceptance tests pass: valid postcode stored/returned, owner without postcode accepted, malformed postcode rejected with 400.",
